# Building a low-compute agent to remove a US household from data brokers

## Executive summary

A practical “removal agent” for a typical US household is a workflow system, not a machine learning system: it keeps a structured inventory of household identifiers (names, phones, emails, addresses, prior addresses), drives broker-specific opt-out steps, records evidence, and rechecks for reappearance on a schedule. The hard parts are not compute, they are friction points such as CAPTCHAs, phone or email verification loops, changing opt-out URLs, and paywalls that hide whether a record is actually gone. citeturn33view1turn50view0turn22view1

The most important US-specific development for minimizing compute is that residents of entity["state","California","us state"] can submit a single deletion request to hundreds of registered data brokers through the state-run Delete Request and Opt-out Platform (DROP). DROP is available to consumers and data brokers must begin processing DROP deletion requests starting August 1, 2026, with a requirement to retrieve and process consumer deletion lists at least every 45 days. citeturn15view1turn45view0turn29view1 This can replace a large portion of broker-by-broker automation for eligible households.

For people-search sites that publish profiles, a low-compute strategy is to prioritize (a) high-visibility sites that appear in multiple “popular” lists, and (b) clusters where one suppression workflow affects multiple brands. One confirmed cluster is the PeopleConnect family (Intelius, US Search, TruthFinder, Instant Checkmate), where opting out routes through a common suppression workflow that includes email and phone code verification and lets the user suppress matching records. citeturn22view1turn25view0turn34view0turn33view0

The recommended architecture is a small local scheduler (cron) plus a light state machine backed by an encrypted SQLite database. It should default to HTTP form submissions and email parsing, and only escalate to a real browser session when a CAPTCHA or JavaScript-only form blocks progress. This keeps compute and energy use low because most runs are short, mostly idle I/O, and do not require long-lived headless browsers.

Unspecified details that affect an implementation include: your state of residence (DROP eligibility), number of household members, name changes (maiden names), number of prior addresses, and whether you will use one shared “opt-out inbox” email address for the whole household. citeturn18view2turn22view1

