## Legal basis and practical opt-out rights in the US

### State privacy rights and data broker laws

US opt-outs are driven mainly by state privacy laws and broker-specific “do not sell/share” or “remove my info” mechanisms. The broadest consumer-facing rights are illustrated by the entity["organization","California Department of Justice","state attorney general office"] summary of the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA): the rights to know, delete, opt out of sale or sharing, correct, and limit certain uses of sensitive personal information, plus non-discrimination for exercising rights. citeturn46view0

Several states require data broker registration, which improves discoverability of brokers and their privacy contacts. For example, entity["state","Oregon","us state"] requires data broker registration and defines “brokered personal data” in a way that explicitly includes data about members of an Oregon resident’s “immediate family or household,” including names and addresses. citeturn35view0 entity["state","Texas","us state"] (through the Texas Data Broker Act) requires registered data brokers to post notices, register, and maintain a comprehensive information security program, with the Texas Secretary of State maintaining a central registry. citeturn35view1turn35view2

entity["organization","California Privacy Protection Agency","state privacy regulator"] now operates a state-hosted deletion and opt-out platform (DROP). DROP allows California residents to submit one deletion request to all active and registered data brokers that are required to process it. citeturn15view0turn15view1

### DROP and why it matters for a low-compute agent

DROP has design features that are relevant to an agent builder because they establish a model for privacy-preserving matching and operational efficiency. The state documentation describes that consumers can submit basic identifiers (name, DOB, ZIP, email, phone) and optionally other identifiers, and that submitted identifiers are hashed so data brokers can match without seeing the original values. citeturn18view2turn18view3turn29view0

The rule text for the “accessible deletion mechanism” further specifies: (a) a “consumer deletion list” contains hashed consumer identifiers and the hashing algorithm used, (b) data brokers must access DROP at least once every 45 calendar days, and (c) subsequent downloads contain only new or amended requests after the last download, which is an incremental update model. citeturn29view0turn29view1

If you are building an agent for your own household, the key consequence is simple: if you are a California resident, you can shift much of the work from “submit dozens of opt-out forms” to “submit one DROP request and track its status,” then focus your compute on a smaller set of public people-search sites and non-covered brokers. citeturn15view1turn18view2turn22view3

### Obstacles and why human-in-the-loop is normal

A recent entity["organization","Joint Economic Committee","us congress committee"] minority report describes practices that reduce opt-out discoverability, including reports of brokers using “no index” instructions that keep opt-out pages out of search results, which adds time and friction for consumers trying to exercise privacy rights. citeturn50view0turn50view1

Independent academic measurement of people-search sites also finds that sites often separate “account data” tools from “public data” tools, impose identity verification steps or paywalls, and have inconsistent responses across users, which complicates automation and verification. citeturn33view0turn33view1

## High-exposure broker landscape and leverage map

### How this report prioritizes brokers by exposure risk

This report prioritizes brokers based on exposure risk for a typical US household defined as: likelihood of appearing in common “people search” results, amount of personal data exposed (addresses, relatives, age/DOB), and impact of one removal action on multiple connected sites. Sites listed as “popular” by Experian and “most common” lists from BrandYourself are weighted up because they are more likely to be encountered by a casual searcher. citeturn22view1turn27view0

### Connection and leverage signals that reduce work

Several cross-site connections reduce the number of distinct workflows you need to run:

- **PeopleConnect cluster (confirmed parent and shared tooling):** A published privacy policy for PeopleConnect describes the operator as The Control Group Media Company LLC, doing business as PeopleConnect, and lists Intelius, US Search, TruthFinder, and Instant Checkmate as sister sites with their own privacy policies and contact emails. citeturn25view0turn25view3 Academic measurement further reports that the “Public Data Tools” across Intelius, Instant Checkmate, TruthFinder and USSearch led to PeopleConnect as the parent company. citeturn33view0

- **Whitepages and 411 linkage (observed and suspected):** Research observed that 411 redirected to parts of Whitepages’ privacy policy and grouped Whitepages and 411 together as connected. citeturn33view0turn33view2

- **PeopleFinders linkage (suspected downstream effects):** The same research reports suspected connected behavior where successful removal from PeopleFinders corresponded with removals observed on TruePeopleSearch and FastPeopleSearch, and summarizes this in an appendix table as “suspected connected sites.” Treat this as a heuristic, not a guarantee, because it is observational and may change. citeturn33view1turn33view2

- **BeenVerified and PeopleLooker linkage (observed response path):** The research reports that emailing PeopleLooker resulted in a response by BeenVerified, indicating an operational connection (shared response path). citeturn33view0turn33view2

These linkages support a low-compute plan: start with the clusters and then mop up remaining high-exposure sites.

